package cajeta.io.net.http;

import cajeta.lang.String;

/**
 * Thrown by the incremental HTTP parser ([`HttpParser`](HttpParser.cajeta),
 * NET-7.3) when the wire bytes are **syntactically not a valid HTTP/1.1
 * message** — a malformed request-line or status-line, a header line
 * with no `:` separator, a non-numeric `Content-Length`, an
 * unparseable status code, and the like.
 *
 * The `position` field carries the 0-based byte offset (into the
 * **accumulated head**, counting from the first byte fed) at which the
 * parser noticed the problem, so a caller can point at the offending
 * character — the same `(message, position)` shape
 * [`MalformedUriException`](../uri/MalformedUriException.cajeta) and
 * [`cajeta.codec.json.JsonParseException`](../../codec/json/JsonParseException.cajeta)
 * carry.
 *
 * ## Hierarchy
 *
 * The spec ([`docs/Net.md`](../../../../../docs/Net.md))
 * places the HTTP parse failures (`MalformedMessage`,
 * `HeadersTooLarge`, `InvalidChunkEncoding`, `UnexpectedEof`) under
 * `cajeta.io.net.NetException` as plan item **NET-7.7**. That item has now
 * landed: this descends from the intermediate
 * [`HttpException`](HttpException.cajeta) → `cajeta.io.net.NetException`
 * root, so it is catchable as `HttpException` (every HTTP parse fault)
 * or the `NetException` root (every networking fault) — exactly as
 * [`MalformedUriException`](../uri/MalformedUriException.cajeta) was
 * reparented for NET-6.5. (NET-7.3 originally rooted this on
 * `cajeta.error.RecoverableException` as a placeholder, because the
 * NET-7.7 base had not been built; NET-7.7 is that one-line reparent.)
 * The NET-7.3 `(message, position)` constructor contract stays
 * **identical** across the reparent; existing call sites and
 * `e.position`-reading catch blocks keep working byte-for-byte.
 *
 * ## The inherited `kind`
 *
 * As a `NetException`, every instance carries the cross-platform
 * [`kind`](../NetException.cajeta) ordinal. A malformed message is an
 * *invalid-input* parse failure, not an errno-derived socket error, so
 * it is classified to
 * [`NetException.KIND_INVALID`](../NetException.cajeta) (12) — the whole
 * HTTP family's bucket, the same one
 * [`MalformedUriException`](../uri/MalformedUriException.cajeta) and
 * `cajeta.codec.json.JsonParseException` use. The precise byte offset
 * still lives in `position`.
 */
public class MalformedMessageException extends HttpException {
    /**
     * Byte offset (0-based) into the accumulated head at which the
     * parser rejected the message. `-1` when the failure is not tied
     * to a single position.
     */
    public int64 position;

    public MalformedMessageException(String message, int64 position) {
        this.message = message;
        this.cause = 0;
        this.kind = 12;   // NetException.KIND_INVALID (via HttpException)
        this.position = position;
    }
}
